{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 28, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "the social network (2010) is a masterfully crafted film that dissects the birth of facebook with razor-sharp precision and biting wit. directed by david fincher, the movie is a hypnotic blend of razor-edged dialogue, sleek visuals, and a pulsing score by trent reznor and atticus ross that drips with tension and ambition. the script by aaron sorkin is a symphony of rapid-fire exchanges, where every line crackles with intelligence, ego, and the intoxicating thrill of revolutionizing an industry.\n\njesse eisenberg’s portrayal of mark zuckerberg is nothing short of electrifying. he captures the genius, arrogance, and social awkwardness of the harvard prodigy with unsettling authenticity. the film doesn’t just humanize zuckerberg—it exposes him as a morally ambiguous figure, a man whose vision outpaces his empathy. andrew Garfield shines as eduardo saverin, the loyal friend turned betrayed ally, delivering a performance that oscillates between vulnerability and quiet fury. justin timberlake’s portrayal of sean parker is a scene-stealing marvel, oozing charisma and menace in equal measure.\n\nthe cinematography by jeff cronenweth is immaculate, bathing the film in a cool, clinical blue hue that mirrors the sterile, high-stakes world of tech startups. the editing is a marvel of pacing, cutting between the frenetic energy of harvard’s campus, the sterile glow of silicon valley offices, and the claustrophobic tension of courtroom battles. fincher’s direction ensures that the film moves like a thriller, even when the subject matter is the abstract concept of code and contracts.\n\nat its core, the social network is a film about betrayal—not just the kind that unfolds in courtrooms, but the quieter, more insidious betrayals of friendship, trust, and ambition. it asks whether genius is justified by its outcomes, and whether the cost of success is worth the price of alienation. the film’s closing lines—“you’re going to go through life thinking girls don’t like you because you’re a nerd. but it’ll be because you’re an asshole”—serve as a biting epitaph for zuckerberg and his creation, a reminder that even the most groundbreaking innovations are built on very human frailties.\n\nif there’s a critique to be made, it’s that the film occasionally feels like a series of vignettes rather than a cohesive narrative, skipping over years in a blink. but this is also its strength—the film mirrors the relentless pace of the digital age, where moments of creation and destruction blur into one continuous feed.\n\nin short, the social network is a near-perfect film, a scathing, stylish, and deeply human portrait of the men who built—and then fractured—the digital world we inhabit. it’s a movie that demands to be watched, rewatched, and dissected, much like the social network itself. 9.5/10.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.3156192898750305}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.0038761973846703768}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " (", "logprob": -0.9602653980255127}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.0006053998949937522}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.4305104514278355e-06}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -6.556489552167477e-06}, {"token": ")", "logprob": -0.7577217221260071}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.1563955545425415}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.022870110347867012}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.6954098343849182}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.5927979350090027}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.027899695560336113}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.6021130084991455}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.25935590267181396}, {"token": " disse", "logprob": -0.5686876177787781}, {"token": "ct", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": "s", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.38785505294799805}, 